00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:36.320
Our AI analysis continues to learn from patterns such as the need for volume confirmation.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:41.100
Adjustments remain focused on requiring multiple factors before entry.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:46.220
Imagine you bought an option spread on a stock the day before its earnings release.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:50.540
The fill came in at a mid price that already reflected elevated IV.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:56.340
What actually happened between your click and that fill price is that market makers widened

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:59.080
the spread to account for the binary event risk.

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:03.940
Implied volatility is the market's expected range priced into options.

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:07.900
When IV ranks sits in the red zone on an earnings screener,

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:12.280
it means the premium is expensive relative to the stock's own history.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:18.500
Selling premium becomes statistically attractive while buying premium requires an even larger move

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:23.340
to overcome the inflated price. Check the IV rank column first.

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:29.220
Then cross reference the expected move against the stock's average post earnings move over the past

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:35.120
eight quarters. If the priced move exceeds the historical average, the edge shifts toward

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:41.640
credit spreads rather than long calls. The biggest misconception is treating all high IV names as

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:48.060
cheap lottery tickets. Always compare the current IV rank to the stock's own 52-week range.

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:54.880
Only sell premium when rank exceeds the 70th percentile. Before we wrap, watch for whether

